/* ============================================================
   Conflicted — service worker
   v2: cache-first APP SHELL + branded offline fallback.

   Why cache-first for the shell: on a resumed/backgrounded PWA,
   iOS reloads the whole page. Network-first made that reload wait
   on the network to re-download index.html before anything painted
   — the multi-second "login screen, then flash, then loading"
   resume jank. We now serve the cached shell INSTANTLY and refresh
   it in the background (stale-while-revalidate), so the app paints
   immediately on resume. Live scores are unaffected: they come from
   /api/* (POST, passed straight through) and data GETs, never the
   HTML document.
   ============================================================ */

const CACHE_VERSION = 'conflicted-v141'; // v141: Onboarding gates on ATTEMPT, not SUCCESS — a blocked platform no longer costs us the account (Matt, 2026-08-01). PROBLEM: finishWizard() blocked on isAnyProviderConnected(), so a Yahoo-only signup hit a hard wall ("Connect at least one platform to continue") and could not create an account at all. Yahoo's fantasy read has been 403 continuously since 7/27 22:12 CT (last ok 7/26 16:17 CT — a pending API-access approval, NOT an outage, see the yahoo memory), so that wall is currently permanent for Yahoo-only users, and it sits directly under the Instagram link-in-bio traffic. Uptime monitor shows only 2 distinct people affected so far (both almost certainly Matt), so this is pre-emptive. DECISION (Matt): selecting a platform stays REQUIRED — it is a real commitment signal and qualifies the funnel — but the connection SUCCEEDING is not, because there is no failure mode where blocking helps: Yahoo down, stale ESPN cookie, mistyped Sleeper username and dead wifi all end with us losing the email, which is the actual asset. One gate rule, three messages; all effort in the copy, none in a fragile classifier. CHANGES: (1) finishWizard() now blocks only on an empty __wizQueue; new wizPendingPlatforms() (chosen but not connected) + cfConnectFailReason(id) returning three honest buckets — platform_paused (Yahoo 403), needs_desktop (ESPN on mobile), incomplete (everything else). (2) Pending platforms recorded to window.__cfPendingPlatforms + localStorage cf_pending_platforms, and onboarding_finished now carries pending/pending_reasons/completed_clean. GOTCHA: only a NON-EMPTY list is persisted — "[]" is truthy in JS and would later divert a fully-connected user whose sessions merely expired. (3) Yahoo-blocked signups POST to the EXISTING /api/integrations/request (platforms:['yahoo-restore'], notify:true, source:'yahoo_outage_signup') so they can be mailed on approval — no new api/*.js, Vercel Hobby is 12/12 FULL. (4) Yahoo is deliberately NOT flagged as unavailable in the picker (Matt's call): OAuth is healthy and only the DATA read is gated, so we still run the full OAuth — a token banked now means those users' leagues light up automatically when Yahoo approves, with no second visit. The 403 is itself proof OAuth succeeded (unauthenticated returns 401), so window.__cfYahooDenied (newly exposed from cfYahooStatus) drives reassuring copy — "Yahoo account linked — leagues are paused... nothing more for you to do" — and promotes the wizard's forward button from ghost-Skip to primary Finish. (5) THREE re-trap paths closed, all of which would have stranded the newly-allowed-through user: handleContinue() used to bare-`return` with no connection (now routes to showConnections()); applyConnectionsAndView() fell back to showSetup() (now keeps an account-holder on Connections); and the boot path at the end of the account-hydration IIFE sent any signed-in user with zero connections into the sign-up wizard on EVERY visit (now diverts to Connections when a pending list exists). (6) New renderPendingConnectionNotice() paints a notice at the top of #view-connections naming the platform they actually picked and why it did not land, self-clearing once connected; amber uses the existing --surf-amber token (--warn-500 was never defined). PREVIOUS — v140: Sentry no longer reports in-app-browser noise as our errors (Matt, 2026-08-01). JAVASCRIPT-5 fired 2026-08-01 17:52 UTC — "TypeError: undefined is not an object (evaluating 'window.webkit.messageHandlers')" from an iPhone on Instagram 440.0.0 / iOS 26.5.2 arriving via the link-in-bio (?utm_source=ig&utm_medium=social&utm_content=link_in_bio&fbclid=...). The stack is sendDataToNative → sendPageHideMessage, neither of which exists anywhere in our codebase — they are Meta's OWN script injected into the page by the Instagram in-app browser, which tries to post a pageHide event back to the native app through the WKWebView bridge and throws when window.webkit.messageHandlers isn't there. Not our bug and not fixable by us; but our reporter is a hand-rolled global window.addEventListener('error') handler, and window.onerror is page-global, so an exception thrown by ANY injected third-party script surfaces under our DSN. Added an IGNORE regex list + isThirdPartyNoise(value, stack, extra) checked at the top of send(), before the dedupe/cap bookkeeping, so noise never consumes a slot in the 25-event cap or the `seen` dedupe map: webkit.messageHandlers, the Meta bridge fns (sendDataToNative/sendPageHideMessage/sendMessageToNative), Android WebView + Chrome-iOS shims (_AutofillCallbackHandler/__gCrWeb/instantSearch), pixels (fbq/ttq/_tfa), and the two classic benign ones (ResizeObserver loop, Non-Error promise rejection captured). Matched against message + stack + extra.source. Deliberately narrow — it is a denylist of known-foreign signatures, NOT a filter on origin, so a real Conflicted error can't be silently swallowed. Expect this to matter more as IG traffic grows: every link-in-bio visitor loads the site inside Meta's wrapper. Version bumped so the Refresh prompt fires. PREVIOUS — v139: Split-wordmark seam normalized site-wide (Matt, 2026-07-31). The bisected CONFLICTED mark is drawn as two clipped <text> halves plus a vertical seam <line>, but the seam's y1/y2/stroke-width had been hand-set per instance instead of derived, so the mark was inconsistent between surfaces: the 72px form (site header, boot splash, impact-animation splash) used y1=14/y2=78/sw=1.4 on baseline 64, which starts the seam ~8 units BELOW the cap height — the line looked flush-topped and stubby instead of passing through the L and running past the letters on both ends. Locked one em-relative spec off the 480x78/F=50/B=54 reference (the 12 share-card + section-header instances that were already correct): y1 = B - 0.84F, y2 = B + 0.24F, stroke-width = 0.024F. Applied: F=72/B=64 -> 3.5 / 81.3 / 1.73 (x3: #boot-splash .boot-wm-seam, .site-header__brand, the ia-splash SVG string); F=56/B=70 -> 22.96 / 83.44 / 1.34 (x2: .menu-drawer__wordmark, the WORDMARK_SVG export constant, both previously 22/82/1.2). All 17 seams in index.html now match. The boot splash's seam animation is transform:scaleY with origin 50% 50%, so it is y-agnostic and unaffected. Also normalized the brand-kit source SVGs (brand-kit/logo/conflicted-wordmark-color.svg + -dark.svg and their Claude Design Training copies); the mono wordmarks have no seam by design. A SPLIT-WORDMARK SEAM SPEC comment now sits above the header wordmark in index.html — derive any new wordmark from those ratios, never eyeball it. Version bumped so the Refresh prompt fires. PREVIOUS — v138: Legal pages rewritten + wired (Matt, 2026-07-31). privacy.html expanded from a short plain-language note into a full policy under the registered entity (Courtney Fryer, LLC d/b/a Conflicted Sports): collection tables, service-provider disclosure (Vercel/Supabase/Resend/Sentry), explicit no-sale/no-share + GPC honoring, cookie/local-storage table, retention table, 13+ COPPA floor, universal access/delete/correct/portability/appeal rights, California CCPA/CPRA notice, EEA/UK legal bases + SCC transfer note, and an Illinois PIPA (815 ILCS 530) breach clause. NEW terms.html: eligibility, platform-connection warranties (you must own the account you connect), acceptable use, a 8-part prohibited-conduct section (unauthorized access, scraping/automation/no-AI-training, security interference, reverse engineering, commercial+competitive misuse, wagering/contest integrity, harassment/doxxing/referral abuse, legal), AS-IS disclaimers, $100 liability cap, indemnification, AAA binding individual arbitration w/ class+jury waiver, mass-filing batching and a 30-day opt-out, Illinois law + DuPage County venue, 1-year limitations, DMCA. index.html: the two placeholder Settings rows (both the signed-in and signed-out Legal & help cards) now navigate to /privacy.html and /terms.html with keyboard handlers; landing footer gained a Terms link. sw.js needs no routing change — the navigate handler already serves any non-shell path network-first, so /terms.html is covered like /privacy.html. Version bumped so the Refresh prompt fires. PREVIOUS — v137: Desktop rail app-icon no longer clipped by the header when the Yahoo outage banner is up (Matt, 2026-07-31). The rail is fixed at top:76px (= header height), but body.cf-outage-on pushes the sticky header DOWN by --cf-outage-h so it occupies --cf-outage-h→--cf-outage-h+76 and overlapped the top of the rail, hiding the split-goalpost mark above GameDay. Added body:not(.nav-landing).cf-outage-on #cf-side-rail { top: calc(76px + var(--cf-outage-h,44px)); } so the rail drops by the banner height and still tucks beneath the header hairline; class-gated so it reverts automatically when the banner clears (no stale offset). index.html-only. v136: Not-yet-built site links hidden from normal users (Matt, 2026-07-31). Shop / Blog / Partners / Contact were rendering to everyone as dimmed, disabled "Soon" entries — dead weight in the nav on both mobile (…More sheet) and desktop (…More sheet + hamburger drawer). They are now owner-only preview items. Mechanism: the …More sheet already had an ADMIN_EMAILS + isAdminUser() + adminOnly-flag filter (built for the Experiment tile), so the four site links just got adminOnly:true — no new gate. The hamburger drawer is static markup, so isAdminUser() is now also mirrored onto body.is-admin (window.cfIsAdmin exposed; synced immediately AND on a 400ms interval, matching the .nav-signedin / experiment-ribbon pattern, because window.__account arrives asynchronously via magic-link return or background /me restore), plus a new pure-CSS hook `.admin-only { display:none !important }` / `body.is-admin .admin-only { display:revert !important }` alongside the existing .exp-only pair. The drawer's Blog + Contact <li>s carry .admin-only (Shop/Partners were never in the drawer and were NOT added). The Experiment tile was ALREADY adminOnly and is unchanged. NOTE: this is a cosmetic client-side filter, not a security boundary — drop the adminOnly flag / .admin-only class on each link as it actually ships. index.html + sw.js only; version bumped so the Refresh prompt fires. v135: Fixed off-center split wordmark on desktop with the left nav rail (Matt, 2026-07-30). Root cause of v128's regression: the desktop rail gives body a padding-left of the rail width (96px) so all in-app content shifts right, but the sticky .site-header is pulled full-bleed (margin-left:-rail + width:100%+rail) so its bottom hairline runs edge-to-edge. That full-bleed also made .site-header__inner (max-width-wide + margin:auto) center on the WHOLE viewport, so the centered split CONFLICTED wordmark sat ~half-a-rail (48px) LEFT of the period bar / scoreboard / all content beneath it. Fix: at ≥900px, added body:not(.nav-landing) .site-header__inner { padding-left: calc(var(--gutter) + var(--cf-rail-w)); } so the inner shares the body's rail-offset coordinate space — the wordmark now centers over the CONTENT column (aligned with .board-inner/.period-bar-inner, all max-width-wide 1320) instead of the screen, on every ≥900px width. Background full-bleed hairline unchanged. index.html-only; version bumped so the Refresh prompt fires. v134: Smoother, longer splash EXIT (Matt, 2026-07-30). The cold-load splash dismissal (triggered by endBoot() removing html.booting) was a 600ms opacity fade + 600ms scale-lift on --ease-out. Extended all three coupled values to 900ms and softened the curve to cubic-bezier(.4,0,.2,1): #boot-splash opacity 600→900ms + its visibility delay 600→900ms (kept equal so the overlay never yanks pointer/visibility before the fade finishes), and .boot-splash__inner transform 600→900ms with the recede scale nudged 1.04→1.06. Entrance keyframes (bootIcIn/bootWmInL/R/bootWmSeam/bootWmUp/bootIcBreathe) untouched — this only affects the leave-to-dashboard transition. index.html-only; version bumped so the Refresh prompt fires. v133: Boot-splash tagline accent recolored to match the wordmark (Matt, 2026-07-30). On the cold-load splash the split CONFLICTED wordmark's coral half renders at --coral-500 (#E88761, the dark-logo coral from conflicted-wordmark-dark.svg — source of truth on dark surfaces), but the "One place." accent right below it used --coral-400 (#F0997B), a lighter coral, so the two coral tones didn't match. Changed .boot-splash__tag .accent from var(--coral-400) → var(--coral-500) so the accent equals both the split wordmark and the logo. Landing hero's "one place." em already used --coral-500 and was unaffected. index.html-only change; version bumped so the Refresh prompt fires. v132: Yahoo outage banner is now AUTO-DETECTING + status logging (Matt, 2026-07-24). The banner no longer shows unconditionally; the Yahoo fetch layer (yhFetch + provider checkStatus) reports every /api/yahoo/data outcome to window.cfYahooStatus(), which raises the banner on a 403 "not authorized" and drops it on the next 200, and logs each status FLIP via cfTrack('yahoo_api_status') for the uptime monitor (readback db/yahoo-api-monitor.sql). Manual dismissal is session-only and auto-re-arms on recovery. Safe to leave in place permanently (inert while Yahoo is 200) — no more hand-toggling per outage. v131: Re-added the TEMP Yahoo API outage banner (Matt, 2026-07-24). Yahoo again dropped the app's Fantasy Sports read permission — runtime logs show /api/yahoo/data returning 403 "This application is not authorized" (most recent ~20:41 UTC 2026-07-24), no 200s, so Yahoo leagues come back empty. Restored the self-contained fixed top banner (style#cf-yahoo-outage-style + div#cf-yahoo-outage + IIFE) as the first block after <body>, session-only dismissal (sessionStorage cfYahooOutageDismissed_v1), body.cf-outage-on reserves the JS-measured --cf-outage-h + parks the sticky header below it. Version bumped so the in-app Refresh prompt fires and users see the banner same-session. REMOVE the whole block once Yahoo restores the read permission (see the v110 removal for reference). v130: ALL desktop phone previews now render at TRUE device scale + WP game-score fix (Matt, 2026-07-24). (1) SNAPSHOT: same regression/fix as WP v127 — the List/Grid previews rendered full cards into a ~286px screen (340px .cf-phone minus padding), so grid tiles clipped off the right edge and list cards crammed. Applied the device-mockup canvas: .cf-phone-screen → fixed 314×628 box (overflow hidden); .cf-snap-frame-d (already wraps wm/kicker/hero/content/foot) became the 390×780 canvas, transform:scale(.805) origin top-left → 314px visual, padding 44/16/16, flex column; wm/kicker/hero/foot flex:none, .cf-snap-content-d flex:1/min-height:0/overflow-y:auto (dropped the max-height:--cf-phone-scroll-max cap). No JS markup change. NOTE: Snapshot KEEPS both List + Grid previews — the 2026-07-23 grid abolition was EXPOSURE's heatmap, not Snapshot. (2) LEADERS: converted the same way — .ldr-phone-screen → fixed 314×628, .ldr-frame-d → 390×780 canvas/flex-col, .ldr-rows flex:1 scroll (was the max-height approach at cramped ~286px). (3) EXPOSURE: same — .exp-phone-screen → fixed 314×628, .exp-frame-d → canvas/flex-col, .expd-list flex:1 (kept the compact expRowsHTML template). (4) WATCH PRIORITY had the canvas since v127 but STILL overran the right edge: the card's fit-to-phone layout lived in @media(max-width:560px) which is VIEWPORT-based, so it never fires inside the 390px canvas on a ≥900px desktop → cards used the wide single-line layout and the kickoff line + yours/vs breakdown ran off-screen. FIX (Matt's idea): moved the yours/vs/conf breakdown OUT of the .wpc__l2 kickoff row into a VERTICAL column on the card's right edge — .wpc__break is now its own flex child of .wpc (flex-direction:column, hairline border-left divider), a sibling of .wpc__main rather than sharing the kickoff line. So the breakdown stacks vertically and NEVER wraps or gets shunted to a second row, on any viewport — no viewport-media dependency at all. .wpc__l2 dropped justify-content:space-between (now just the when-line, .wpc__when flex:1 ellipsis); removed the @media(max-width:560px) wrap block AND the v130 .wpd-list wrap overrides (both obsolete). JS: renderWpCard emits .wpc__break as a sibling after .wpc__main. Applies to the real mobile card too so the preview stays faithful (no drift). ALSO per Matt: removed the NFL game score (e.g. "BAL 20–27 KC") from the WP desktop preview only — gated renderWpCard's score whenBit on !state._preview; the real mobile card still shows it. WP card copy/layout (Matt, 2026-07-24): (a) breakdown chip labels renamed yours→Cheer, vs→Enemy, conf→Conflicted; (b) the "Game of the Week" chip moved out of the .wpc__l1 matchup row into its own banner ROW at the TOP of the card — .wpc is now a flex COLUMN (gap:8px) wrapping an optional .wpc__gotw-banner (full-width CENTERED coral strip, dark text, matches the GOTW slot header) + the main .wpc__row (number·matchup·breakdown); non-GOTW cards have a single .wpc__row child so they render unchanged. GOTW card is ~1 row taller than the rest by design. (c) removed the coral slot-header treatment for the GOTW's time slot (e.g. "Sunday Early") — dropped the wp-slot--gotw class from the slot section + deleted its 5 CSS rules; the GOTW is now identified solely by the card banner, so its slot header matches every other slot. (d) breakdown column .wpc__break given a FIXED width:112px (was content-sized flex:none) so the divider + chips start at the same x on every card — a longer "Conflicted" label no longer shifts that card's column left vs Cheer/Enemy-only cards; the rare zero-player fallback text shortened "No players in this game"→"No players" to fit the fixed column. v129: Watch Priority period bar unified into the shared #cf-period-bar (Matt, 2026-07-23). WP was the last main view keeping its OWN in-view period control (.wp-head__controls: duplicate week/season selects + arrows + handlers in the navy surf palette). Retired it — WP now drives the shared #cf-period-bar exactly like Exposure: cfShowPeriodBar tags the bar .pb-watch (shows the pb-view-head title "Watch priority" + subtitle, text set per-view so it never shows Exposure's heading), the shared week/season selects+arrows now route through window.changeWeek/changeWeekTo/changeSeason (added 'changeWeek' to the WP refetch wrapper) so changing period from the shared bar fires WP's schedule refetch+rerender, and the shared Filters button routes to the WP settings sheet via window.__wpOpenFilters (cfOpenHeaderFilters + cfSyncHeaderFilters WP branches, Filters always shown). CSS: .pb-watch shows pb-view-head; body.wp-active hides the old inline .wp-head; desktop Mobile-Preview hides the shared bar title (copy column carries its own headline) while the desktop :not() rule already centers+constrains the bar to 600px. The old .wp-head__controls markup stays in the DOM (hidden) so renderWatchView's existing sync calls don't null-error. v128: Desktop left nav rail (Matt, 2026-07-23). Replaced the desktop hamburger with a persistent left-side rail (the mobile bottom bar's vertical twin) at ≥900px for in-app users — gated to body:not(.nav-landing), the SAME gate the mobile bar uses, so the landing/setup page keeps its hamburger for signed-out visitors. New #cf-side-rail markup + <style> (fixed left rail, top:76px below the sticky header, body padding-left:96px to clear content, hamburger hidden in-app on desktop) and a forwarding script: rail buttons trigger the matching hidden #exp-bottom-nav button's click so navigation stays single-source, and a 350ms poll mirrors the live view onto the rail's is-on highlight. Having vertical room, the rail promotes Exposure + Schedule (Watch Priority) out of the …More sheet into first-class tabs (GameDay·Snapshot·Leaders·Conflict·Exposure·Schedule + Account·More); the now-redundant Exposure/Schedule/Account tiles are hidden from the …More sheet on desktop only (mobile keeps them), while the not-yet-functional site links (Shop/Blog/Partners/Contact) stay in …More. The rail's top mark is the real split-goalpost app icon (/icons/export/icon-1a-night.png, night variant) as a home link — not a hand-drawn placeholder. Mobile bottom bar (≤768px) untouched. v127: Watch Priority phone previews now render at TRUE device scale (Matt, 2026-07-23). The v126 previews squeezed full game cards into a ~286px screen, so everything wrapped and looked crammed / out of scale vs the real iPhone. Switched to the proper device-mockup technique: wpPhoneInner wraps content in a .wp-phone-canvas rendered at real iPhone width (390px) and the whole canvas is transform:scale(.805) → 314px to fill a fixed 314×628 .wp-phone-screen, so cards keep their genuine on-device proportions (single-line breakdown, real font sizes). Canvas is a flex column: wordmark/kick/hero pinned (flex:none), .wpd-list flex:1 fills the phone height + scrolls internally (interactivity still blocked on the cards, not the list). Removed the v126 compact/wrap overrides (.wpc__l2 wrap, .wpc__when normal, num shrink) — no longer needed now that cards have real width. Bumped wordmark 150→168 / kicker 9→11 / hero 14→17 to match on-device sizing. v126: Desktop Watch Priority preview fixes (Matt, 2026-07-23). (1) Layout was overflowing: the 3-column row (copy + two 340px phones ≈ 1240px) didn't fit the view's 980px container, so it wasn't centered — copy clipped off the left, 2nd phone off the right, card content cut by the viewport. Fix: in Mobile Preview #view-watch widens to --max-width-wide (1320px) with the period controls kept centered+constrained to 600px, and the stack breakpoint moved 1199→1319px so it only goes 3-across when there's room (≥1320px); 900–1319px stacks copy-on-top + two phones side by side. (2) Scroll was dead because .wpd-list had pointer-events:none (which also kills wheel + scrollbar drag) — moved the interaction-block onto the cards/slot-headers/pills instead so the list scrolls (capped at --cf-phone-scroll-max 440px) but clicks still don't open drawers/toggle slots. (3) Game cards overflowed the ~286px phone horizontally (the card's own ≤380px wrap rule never fires at desktop widths) — added .wpd-list compact overrides so .wpc__l2 wraps + .wpc__when goes multi-line + metrics tighten. (4) Column-3 caption renamed "Cheer watch"→"Your Guys Only"; removed the "with Hate Watch" phrase from the copy column. v125: Desktop Watch Priority = Mobile Preview / Desktop View toggle (Matt, 2026-07-23). At ≥900px Watch Priority now defaults to the standard phone-frame Mobile Preview — a framed informative copy column beside TWO phone previews: "Maximum investment" (center, coral cap) and "Cheer watch" (right, green cap). Hate Watch is intentionally NOT previewed; it stays reachable in Desktop View. Both phones reuse the SAME slot markup as the live list: renderWatchView's slot-building was extracted into buildWatchSlots() (returns {html}); watchPreviewHTML(view) temp-swaps state.view + forces mustWatch:false, builds, restores, and strips element ids + the collapsed class so the preview can't collide with the live #wp-slots DOM or render folded (interactivity off via .wpd-list pointer-events). Badge/state-cache writes are skipped during a preview build (state._preview) so live Filters counts aren't clobbered. A new Filters→Layout toggle (Mobile Preview ⇄ Desktop View, ≥900px only) swaps to the current functional list unchanged; the choice PERSISTS (localStorage cf_watch_viewmode) since it's a layout pref not a content-hiding filter. In Mobile Preview the now-redundant View toggle is hidden (phones are fixed Max+Cheer); it returns in Desktop View (all three views incl. Hate). Copy column has the "Built for mobile · Email me the link" nudge → wpEmailLink() POSTs the account email to /api/auth/magic/request (demo hides button+divider+hint), fires cfTrack('watch_mobile_link_sent'). WP owns its own .wp-head controls (not the shared period bar) so no period-bar override needed; the page title is hidden in Mobile Preview. Mobile Watch Priority (<900px) untouched. v124: Watch Priority — (1) Filter scope toggle reordered so All games is on the LEFT, Must Watch Games on the right. (2) Default view changed from Maximum Investment to Cheer Watch (initialView + reset default; a previously-saved view choice in localStorage still wins for returning users) (Matt, 2026-07-23). v123: Removed the per-card tier text labels (Must watch / High / On your radar / Skip) from Watch Priority cards — the accent color still signals priority; only the Game of the Week keeps its label (Matt, 2026-07-23). v122: Fix — Must Watch filter appeared stuck on All games (Matt, 2026-07-23). The v121 card rewrite changed Must Watch to show every game with a player, which (since you have players in most games) looked almost identical to All games. Restored the original semantic: Must Watch = the single top game in each time window; All games = the full slate (zero-player games dimmed). v121: Watch Priority card redesign (Matt, 2026-07-23). Replaced the tall spotlight + strip cards with ONE compact low-height card (.wpc) for every game: tier-colored accent bar + priority number + matchup/tier line + kickoff/network + yours/against/conflicted breakdown dots (derived from the same s.players the number uses). Inline player pills removed — players are seen via the existing tap → game drawer (data-wp-game hook + role=button preserved). Accordion time-window slots kept; every game now renders as a compact card sorted by score (All games shows the full slate w/ zero-player games dimmed; Must Watch shows only games with players). GOTW card gets the coral 'Game of the Week' accent + keeps its coral slot header. Dark-only. Defaults unchanged (view=maximum, All games). renderSpotlight/renderStripRow/pillsHtmlFor kept but no longer called. v120: Desktop period-bar polish (Matt, 2026-07-23). (1) Watch Priority owns its OWN control frame (.wp-head__controls, navy surf palette) not the shared #cf-period-bar, so v119's :not() rule missed it — now centred+constrained to 600px directly (align-self:center) so it matches the others. (2) Added breathing room below the period bar on desktop: #cf-period-bar .period-bar-inner padding-bottom 14px (dashboard/exposure/snapshot/leaders) + #view-watch .wp-head margin-bottom 24px. v119: Desktop — brought the GameDay/dashboard + Watch Priority period bar to the new standard: the plain one-row bar (no pb-* view class) now centres + constrains its week/season nav-controls to 600px at ≥900px, matching Snapshot/Leaders/Exposure. Scoped via #cf-period-bar:not(.pb-leaders):not(.pb-snapshot):not(.pb-exposure) so the three special views keep their own rules; mobile untouched (Matt, 2026-07-23). v118: (1) Moved Exposure's position filters OUT of the period bar seg row INTO the Filters sheet, placed last under the league list (new "Position" section, pills wrap via .exp-filters--sheet, reuse data-exp-pos handler + updateSettingsSheetUI sync). Exposure period bar is now a single-row frame (removed seg-row display/merge/divider; nav-controls keeps its own border, still centered+constrained to 600px on desktop). (2) Removed the global "Show all leagues" reset button from the shared cfRenderLeagueSection() — re-enabling a hidden league is per-row now (Matt, 2026-07-23). v117: Added a divider line between the Exposure period bar's two rows — row 1 (week/year/Filters) and row 2 (position pills) — via border-top:1px var(--line) on #cf-period-bar.pb-exposure .pb-seg-row (Matt, 2026-07-23). v116: Exposure Mobile Preview polish (Matt, 2026-07-23). (1) Hid the page-level "Exposure map" title + subtitle (.pb-view-head) in Mobile Preview only — redundant above the two labelled phones; returns in Desktop View. (2) Centered the position-filter pill strip in the period bar (justify-content:safe center on #exp-filters). (3) Fixed the bar-graph sliver in the phone previews: the functional list measures a fixed name column off the widest name, which swallowed the whole bar column inside the ~282px phone screen — phone rows now use a compact flexible template (name minmax(0,1.35fr) ellipsized + bar minmax(34px,1fr) + 30px count) via a new `compact` arg to expRowsHTML. v115: Desktop Exposure = Mobile Preview / Desktop View toggle (Matt, 2026-07-23). The Exposure grid was already gone (list-only); at ≥900px Exposure now defaults to the standard phone-frame Mobile Preview — a framed informative copy column beside TWO phone previews: "On my rosters" (center, coral) and "Against me" (right, blue), the old Positive/Negative mode toggle unfolded into the two columns. Both phones reuse computeExposureData(mode)/expRowsHTML so they can't drift from the real leaderboard; players on BOTH sides get a gold conflicted edge. .exp-phone-screen carries an always-dark palette so the shared .exp-list markup renders correctly on the dark phone regardless of page theme. A new Filters→Layout toggle (Mobile Preview ⇄ Desktop View, ≥900px only) swaps to the current functional leaderboard; the choice PERSISTS (localStorage cf_exposure_viewmode) since it's a layout pref not a content-hiding filter — a user who picks Desktop View stays there (may have phone+desktop open). In Mobile Preview the now-redundant Mode toggle is hidden; it returns in Desktop View. Copy column has the "Built for mobile · Email me the link" nudge → expEmailLink() POSTs the account email to /api/auth/magic/request (demo hides button+divider+hint), fires cfTrack('exposure_mobile_link_sent'). Exposure period bar brought to the new standard: nav-controls + position-pill seg row centered+constrained to 600px (pills kept — they filter both phones — unlike Snapshot/Leaders which hide their seg row). Mobile Exposure (<900px) untouched. v114: Standardized desktop phone-preview scroll cap (Matt, 2026-07-23). Introduced a shared :root token --cf-phone-scroll-max (440px): the scrollable list region of any phone-framed desktop board caps at this height and scrolls internally so the phone stays a natural height instead of stretching the page. Applied to BOTH the Snapshot content (.cf-snap-content-d) and Leaders rows (.ldr-frame-d .ldr-rows) — replaced Leaders' earlier fixed-height(620)+flex approach (which shrank+clipped rows) with the cleaner max-height-on-scroll-region pattern; wordmark/hero/total stay pinned above, Snapshot foot stays below. .ldr-rows is a column flexbox so its children get flex:0 0 auto to keep natural height (each .ldr-row is overflow:hidden and would otherwise clip). Short Snapshot lists render naturally shorter; the cap only bites when content would stretch the phone. Future phone-framed views (Conflict Card next) should reuse --cf-phone-scroll-max. v113: Desktop Leaders = 3-column feature layout, mirroring the Snapshot treatment (Matt, 2026-07-23). At ≥900px the Leaders view drops the mobile single card/Carried-Crushed toggle/share button and shows an informative framed COPY column ("Who carried you. Who crushed you.") beside two phone-framed boards — Carried Me (center) and Crushed Me (right) — painted by render()/paintLdrDesktop()/ldrFrameInner() from the same rows as mobile (parametrized by side F/A instead of st.side), always showing ALL rows. Column captions colored green (Carried) / red (Crushed). Nudge = "Built for mobile · Email me the link" → ldrEmailLink() POSTs the signed-in account email to /api/auth/magic/request; demo hides button+divider+hint. Each board in a .ldr-phone-frame edge frame, notch-clearing top padding, responsive (≥1200 three cols / 900–1199 copy on top + 2 boards / <900 mobile). The shared period bar is centered+constrained to 600px and its Carried/Crushed toggle row hidden on desktop Leaders (override extended to .pb-leaders, placed after the shared rules for source order). NEW: Leaders added to the hamburger menu (Views section, shows in demo) — it was previously unreachable on desktop since the bottom nav is mobile-only (≤768px) and Leaders wasn't in the hamburger. Mobile Leaders untouched. v112: Desktop Weekend Snapshot = 3-column feature layout (Matt, 2026-07-23). At ≥900px the Snapshot view drops the mobile single card/toggle/share button and shows an informative framed COPY column beside two phone-framed List + Grid previews, painted by the same render()/buildRows/cardHTML/tileHTML so the preview can't drift from the real cards. Copy is informative (framed around checking/managing leagues), with a "Built for mobile · Email me the link" nudge that POSTs the signed-in account email to /api/auth/magic/request (one-click login link); demo mode hides the email button+divider+hint (no account). Each phone wrapped in .cf-phone-frame (navy-line edge frame matching the copy frame), notch-clearing top padding so the wordmark clears the decorative notch. Responsive: ≥1200 three columns / 900–1199 copy stacks on top + 2 phones side by side / <900 mobile view. The shared period bar is centered + constrained to 600px on desktop Snapshot and its List/Grid toggle row hidden — the override is placed AFTER the shared .pb-snapshot rules (~L16959) so it wins source order (an earlier-in-file rule silently loses). Weekend Snapshot menu item de-gated from menu-signedin-only so it shows in the desktop demo hamburger. Mobile Snapshot untouched. v111: Account > League Connections — Yahoo connect no longer full-page-redirects (Matt, 2026-07-22). Clicking "Connect to Yahoo" from the Account view used to run window.location.href='/api/auth/yahoo', which navigated the whole SPA away; after OAuth the callback cold-boots the app at '/', and during a Yahoo hiccup (e.g. the read-permission outage) that reboot dumped the signed-in user on the logged-out landing page. Now the Account view reuses the wizard's new-tab OAuth (?wizard=1 self-closing callback) and polls /api/yahoo/data from the page (connectionsConnectYahoo/pollConnectionsYahoo) so the user never leaves Account: 200=connected, 401=still finishing OAuth (keep polling ~75s), 403=Yahoo declined (outage) → inline plain-language detail message on the Yahoo card. Poll stopped + state cleared on view enter/exit. Pre-login setup grid keeps the old full-page redirect (landing is fine there). v110: Removed the TEMP Yahoo API outage banner (Matt, 2026-07-22). Yahoo restored our Fantasy Sports read permission app-side — runtime logs confirm /api/yahoo/data returning 200s with real league data as of ~20:20 UTC, no more 403 "This application is not authorized". Deleted the whole self-contained banner block (style#cf-yahoo-outage-style + div#cf-yahoo-outage + IIFE) that sat right after <body> in index.html; <body> now flows straight into the Boot splash again. Version bumped so the in-app Refresh prompt fires and users stop seeing the banner same-session. v109: TEMP Yahoo API outage banner (Matt, 2026-07-22). Yahoo dropped our Fantasy Sports read permission app-side, so all Yahoo league data is coming back empty. Added a self-contained, dismissible fixed top banner (first block after <body> in index.html, --surf-* chrome palette + amber pulse dot) that shows on every screen incl. the Yahoo connect flow, regardless of whether the user has a Yahoo league. Dismissal is session-only (sessionStorage cfYahooOutageDismissed_v1) so it returns next visit while the outage persists. body.cf-outage-on reserves --cf-outage-h (JS-measured) padding-top and parks the sticky header just below the banner. REMOVE the whole TEMP block + this note once the Yahoo app permission is restored. v108: Hero CTA buttons no longer stretch full-width on mobile (Matt, 2026-07-22). flex:1 1 0 was making each button fill half the row (too wide); switched the ≤640px primary row to flex:0 0 auto with a shared min-width:136px — sized to the wider "Get started" label + padding — so both stay equal and the centered pair sits at natural width instead of edge-to-edge. Still nowrap/side-by-side. v107: Hero CTA pair stays side-by-side on mobile (Matt, 2026-07-22). At ≤640px the two 210px-min buttons were wrapping to two rows; added a mobile rule so the primary row (.lp-cta-row:not(--secondary)) is flex-wrap:nowrap with the buttons flex:1 1 0 / min-width:0 — they now drop the fixed width and share the row, shrinking to fit any phone width. Desktop symmetry (210px min-width) unchanged; demo/ghost row unaffected. v106: Sign-in modal decluttered (Matt, 2026-07-22). Removed the "or / Get started / Sign up" section from openSignInModal() — the modal is now sign-in only (email + magic-link button). Dropped the espn-modal-or divider, the Get started heading, the #signin-signup button, and its orphaned click handler (which would have thrown on the now-missing element). New users still reach the funnel via the hero "Get started" button; no need to repeat it here. v105: Hero pair made symmetrical + sign-in spam note (Matt, 2026-07-22). (1) Sign in / Get started now render equal width — min-width:210px + centered text on .lp-cta-row:not(--secondary) .btn-hero — so the two-button row is symmetrical regardless of label length; demo/ghost row exempt. (2) Added a brand-tone spam-folder line to the "Check your email" confirmation in openSignInModal(): "No link within 10 seconds? Check your spam folder — it likes to hide there sometimes." v104: Landing hero CTA split into a two-button row for returning users (Matt, 2026-07-22). Added a "Sign in" button to the LEFT of "Get started", colored to match the split wordmark — .btn-hero--signin uses var(--navy-700) (#7AA8D1, the blue left half) with the same dark #0F1B26 label and 15px/28px padding as the coral --primary (var(--coral-500), the right half); hover lightens navy via color-mix. Sign in wires to openSignInModal() (magic-link path for existing users), Get started keeps scrollToGetStarted(). This surfaces sign-in that was previously buried behind the hamburger → "Sign in" item. The "Try demo mode" ghost button moved to a second .lp-cta-row--secondary row below the pair (12px gap), so the primary row is the clean Sign in / Get started pair. v103: Blue frame added around the app icon in the PWA install drawer — 1.5px --navy-500 border on .cf-install__icon, box-sizing:border-box so the tile stays 52px (Matt, 2026-07-22). v102: Snapshot share bar widened to match Leaders (Matt, 2026-07-22). The mobile bottom share bar was a different width on Snapshot (420px cap) vs Leaders (520px cap). Since Leaders' bar lines up perfectly with its card frame, brought Snapshot to match: .cf-snap-wrap max-width 420→520 and the fixed .cf-snap-share width min() cap 420→520, so the Snapshot card + share bar now equal Leaders' 520px and the bar stays flush with the framing on both views. v101: Scoreboard/Rooting Card share icon brightened to full --ink-900 (was --ink-500) with a heavier stroke (2.75), matching the section wordmark weight (Matt, 2026-07-22). v100: PWA install prompt moved from floating banner to the shared BottomSheet drawer (Matt, 2026-07-22). Same 30s post-dashboard dwell trigger and all gating (in-app/demo/standalone checks, beforeinstallprompt capture) unchanged, but presentation now reuses window.BottomSheet (owner 'pwa-install') so it matches the app's other drawers — grab handle, .55 backdrop, scroll-lock. Content: home-screen icon (icon-192), reordered benefits (Full screen, no browser bar / One tap from your home screen / No password to remember), Android real one-tap Install (deferredPrompt.prompt via .btn-primary) with "Not now", iOS shows browser-aware Share → Add to Home Screen steps with "Got it". Naggy by design: dismissal only suppresses for the current session (sessionDismissed flag), no 30-day localStorage persistence — re-summons every visit. Old #conflicted-install-banner element, its <style>, and the tab-bar offset rule removed. v99: Priority onboarding deferred to returning users (Matt, 2026-07-22). The "rank your leagues" screen no longer auto-launches on a user's first visit — they now get to land on the dashboard, see their connected leagues, and look around first. shouldAutoLaunchPriority now records the first dashboard visit (localStorage gd_priority_first_seen) and tags the session that created it (sessionStorage gd_priority_first_session); the prompt only fires once they return in a LATER session with priorities still unset. First-visit renders/refreshes within that first session no longer trigger it. Demo mode, the <2-league guard, isPrioritySet(), and the Settings "Re-rank" path are all unchanged. v98: Rooting-direction dots extended to ALL loyalty cards (Matt, 2026-07-20). Cheer/Enemy card league-row dots now use the same semantic colors as Conflicted cards — green (--cheer) = your roster, red (--enemy) = opponent's — replacing league-color dots everywhere in the buckets; the 0.5 dim on opponent rows is gone since red carries the signal. v97: Loyalties buckets made strictly disjoint (Matt, 2026-07-20). User feedback: conflicted players appearing under Cheer, Enemy, AND Conflicted tabs was confusing. Removed the mobile-only concat that surfaced conflicted players (gold-framed) inside the Cheer/Enemy lists — they now live ONLY in the Conflicted tab, on every viewport. To preserve the rooting guidance those duplicate placements gave, conflicted cards' league-row dots are now rooting-direction dots: green (--cheer) where the player is on YOUR roster in that league, red (--enemy) where your opponent has him — replacing the league-color dots on conflicted cards only. Enemy-side rows on conflicted cards drop the 0.5 dim so the red reads full-strength. Cheer/Enemy cards keep league-color dots, unchanged. v96: ESPN renewal-status probe, diagnostic only (Matt, 2026-07-20). ESPN has no clean renewal signal (Sleeper/Yahoo pending-league discovery can't cover it), so before designing detection we capture what ESPN's payload actually says: new lean `probe` endpoint on api/espn/data.js (views mSettings+mStatus+mDraftDetail+mNav — no rosters/boxscore), client fires it once per league+season per session on live-period loads (this season AND the prior one for a renewed-vs-carryover comparison), 4s off the render path, deduped, fire-and-forget. Fields (status.isActive/previousSeasons/teamsJoined/dates, draftDetail.drafted/picks, draftSettings date/type/keeperCount, schedule shape, seasonId echo, non-200 statuses) go out via cfTrack('espn_renewal_probe') — readback SQL in db/espn-renewal-probe.sql — and mirror to window.__espnProbe. Zero behavior change; remove the block once detection ships.  League show/hide races + Rooting Card leak (Matt, 2026-07-20). (1) Rooting Card now applies the shared hiddenLeagues filter: players from Filters-hidden leagues are dropped from the card, and every count (hero numbers, ×N flanks, sort weights, the 'N leagues' sub-line) is computed from VISIBLE leagues only — players are cloned so __conflictedLastData stays pristine for the dashboard. (2) Race A self-heal: loadData stamps each request with a signature (season|week|providers#disabled) and its coalescing guard now settles through __settleSelfHeal — if the user changed period or toggled a league while a fetch flew (toggle-off → week change → toggle-on), one fresh load is chained and the stale result is discarded, never rendered. (3) Race B cache poisoning: the historic-cache key is captured at request time (__reqKey) alongside a snapshot of the disabled set (__reqDisabled, used for the post-fetch filter), so a mid-flight toggle can no longer file differently-filtered data under the wrong key. v94: Matchup drawer — team abbreviations removed + "Player 12345" eliminated (Matt, 2026-07-20). (1) Dropped the NFL team abbreviation line under each player name in the paired rows (the matchup-drawer__player-team span in halfCells); the centered POS chips are untouched. (2) Fixed raw "Player <id>" labels appearing when a Sleeper matchup was opened during a cold open. Root cause: Sleeper stores raw player IDs and the drawer resolves names from window.playerDB at OPEN time, but on a snapshot boot the board is clickable before that ~5MB DB loads (Yahoo/ESPN embed the name, so were never affected; the canonical Sleeper-ID mapping is a separate build-time layer and isn't involved). Three-part fix: (a) the boot IIFE now hydrates window.playerDB synchronously from its localStorage cache BEFORE the snapshot paints/becomes clickable, deliberately IGNORING the 12h CACHE_TTL — the TTL governs when to refetch, but a stale DB renders names fine, and honoring it skipped the hydrate for anyone opening a day later (most cold opens); loadData still refetches and overwrites. (b) new rerenderOpenMatchupDrawer() repaints an open drawer in place once loadData sets window.playerDB, self-healing without a close+reopen. (c) normalizePlayer now splits the two ways an ID lookup comes back empty and never renders the raw ID for either: DB-not-loaded-yet = TRANSIENT → new `pending` flag renders the app's standard .skel shimmer bar in the name slot (84px / 68px mobile, reduced-motion inherited); DB-loaded-but-ID-missing = PERMANENT (new signing / stale cache) → neutral "Unknown player", since a shimmer there would spin forever. v93: Removed the doubled header above the league toggles (Matt, 2026-07-19). The shared cfRenderLeagueSection() partial already renders its own 'League / Show' column head, but both host sheets were wrapping it in another heading — Leaders in <h4>LEAGUES</h4> and Exposure in a 'Leagues' settings label — so the control read as two stacked headers saying nearly the same word. Dropped both wrappers and kept the shared column head, since that one also labels the Show column.
const OFFLINE_URL = '/offline.html';
const APP_SHELL_URL = '/'; // canonical cache key for the single-page shell

/* Files cached at install so the shell + offline page work instantly,
   even before the user has ever loaded the site online. */
const PRECACHE_ASSETS = [
  '/',
  '/index.html',
  '/offline.html',
  '/icons/icon-192.png',
  '/icons/icon-512.png',
  '/brand-kit/logo/conflicted-wordmark-color.svg'
];

/* ---- Install: pre-cache the shell + offline page + brand assets ----
   NOTE: we deliberately do NOT call skipWaiting() here. On an UPDATE (an old
   SW is still controlling open tabs) the new SW installs and then WAITS, so the
   page can show a "new version available — tap to refresh" prompt and activate
   it on the user's command (see the SKIP_WAITING message handler below). On a
   first-ever install there's no controller to replace, so it activates normally
   and clients.claim() (in activate) takes control of the open page. */
self.addEventListener('install', (event) => {
  event.waitUntil(
    caches.open(CACHE_VERSION)
      // Individual puts so one missing asset can't fail the whole install.
      .then((cache) => Promise.all(
        PRECACHE_ASSETS.map((url) =>
          cache.add(url).catch((e) => console.warn('precache skip', url, e && e.message))
        )
      ))
  );
});

/* ---- Activate the waiting worker on demand (user tapped "Refresh") ---- */
self.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
  if (event.data && event.data.type === 'SKIP_WAITING') self.skipWaiting();
});

/* ---- Activate: clean up old caches from prior versions ---- */
self.addEventListener('activate', (event) => {
  event.waitUntil(
    caches.keys().then((keys) =>
      Promise.all(
        keys.filter((k) => k !== CACHE_VERSION).map((k) => caches.delete(k))
      )
    ).then(() => self.clients.claim())
  );
});

/* Revalidate the shell in the background: fetch a fresh copy and store it
   so the NEXT load gets the update. Failures are swallowed (offline etc.). */
function revalidateShell(req) {
  return fetch(req).then((res) => {
    if (res && res.ok) {
      const copy = res.clone();
      caches.open(CACHE_VERSION).then((cache) => cache.put(APP_SHELL_URL, copy)).catch(() => {});
    }
    return res;
  }).catch(() => null);
}

/* ---- Fetch ---- */
self.addEventListener('fetch', (event) => {
  const req = event.request;

  // Only handle GET; let everything else (POST to /api/*) pass through.
  if (req.method !== 'GET') return;

  if (req.mode === 'navigate') {
    const path = new URL(req.url).pathname;
    // Only the single-page app shell is served cache-first. Standalone pages
    // (e.g. /privacy.html) must NOT be replaced by the app shell — they stay
    // network-first with cache fallback.
    const isAppShell = (path === '/' || path === '/index.html');

    if (isAppShell) {
      // CACHE-FIRST shell + background revalidate. Paints instantly on resume.
      // If the shell isn't cached yet (first ever visit), fall back to network,
      // then the offline page. Note: a 401/403 is NOT an offline condition — the
      // user is online but their session expired; the app renders its own
      // reconnect state from the cached shell.
      event.respondWith(
        caches.match(APP_SHELL_URL).then((cached) => {
          if (cached) {
            revalidateShell(req); // refresh cache for next time, don't block paint
            return cached;
          }
          return fetch(req)
            .then((res) => {
              if (res && res.ok) {
                const copy = res.clone();
                caches.open(CACHE_VERSION).then((cache) => cache.put(APP_SHELL_URL, copy)).catch(() => {});
              }
              return res;
            })
            .catch(() => caches.match(OFFLINE_URL));
        })
      );
      return;
    }

    // Other pages: network-first, fall back to cache, then offline page.
    event.respondWith(
      fetch(req).catch(() => caches.match(req).then((c) => c || caches.match(OFFLINE_URL)))
    );
    return;
  }

  // Other GETs (icons, fonts, brand assets): cache-first, fall back to network.
  event.respondWith(
    caches.match(req).then((cached) => cached || fetch(req))
  );
});
